Coders Strike Back (Mad Pod Racing): the rules of the game CodinGame runs today, in its top league (Legend), on
"Referees/Coders Strike Back/Referee.java" of https://github.com/SpiritusSancti5/codinGame (c0cc6fe), an older version
of the game (no BOOST, other laps...). Applied by legacy/build.sh for the output name cg-2016-coders-strike-back; the file as
published is built as cg-2016-coders-strike-back-original.

Inferred from CodinGame's public replays (gameResult/findByGameId: 364 Legend games, 300 Gold), which the jar built
with this patch reproduces frame for frame (views, infos, summaries, tooltips, scores) from the seed alone. The replays
show what the live referee does, not its code: this patch is one way to write it, and only the behaviour the replays
show is certain. Each change and its evidence:
  - BOOST: the first BOOST of a pod is a thrust of 650 ("boosts towards (x, y)" in the infos), later ones the maximum
    thrust (MAX_POWER), during a shield cooldown 0 like any thrust; a BOOST during a cooldown also uses the boost up
    (6 replays). Each pod has its own (454 players had both pods at 650).
  - "its" instead of "it's" in the two shield action texts.
  - 3 laps whatever the number of checkpoints (the file: (int) (15.0 / count): 5 laps of 3, 2 of 6).
  - CHECKPOINT_GENERATION_MAX_GAP 30 (the file: 100): with it, java.util.Random(seed) gives the map of every replay.
  - The time of a checkpoint is the round plus the fraction of the round where the pod's path (its segments between
    collisions, as the file checks them) enters the checkpoint's circle (the file: the round). Seen in the
    eliminations (a player whose last checkpoint was at round r is out at r + 101; without any checkpoint at 100) and
    in the view's timeout counter (101 after a pod ends a round exactly 600 from its checkpoint). The formula of the
    fraction is this patch's; the replays only show its integer part and that it is above 0.
  - A pod that finished the race keeps 0 as its next checkpoint (its count capped at the race's, in the view).
  - Two pods with as many checkpoints, on their last leg or finished, rank by the time of their last checkpoint,
    earlier first (on the other legs the distance to the next checkpoint, as in the file).
The lower leagues differ by constants not changed here (Gold: MAX_POWER 100; Silver and below: one pod per player, other
maps, MAX_POWER 100, whole rounds as checkpoint times; Wood: no pod collisions).

